    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the info. i'm worried about the \"ordinary and necessary\" part though. since i work from home, sometimes it feels like the line between my personal stuff and my work stuff gets super blurry. like, if i buy a desk lamp that i also use for reading at night, is that gonna be a problem for an audit? i don't want anything to look like i'm hiding stuff or being shady with my deductions.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When claiming home office expenses, the IRS usually expects you to substantiate that the items are indeed ordinary (common in your line of work) and necessary (appropriate and helpful for your business). In the case of the desk lamp you mentioned, you've indicated that it serves a dual purpose. However, if it's part of your essential working conditions, it can still qualify as a business expense even if it serves a personal function. The challenge here is to prove it’s used significantly for business. Documentation, like comparing its business-related usage versus personal use or maybe keeping logs that show the lamp being on during business hours, can help. Having such dual-purpose items deemed as business expenses usually won't be problematic, provided they're justified and documented correctly. Again, seeking advice from a tax advisor can assist in determining the best way to handle such ambiguities and ensure that you do it within the IRS regulations.",
      "source": "target",
